WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:08.340
One big mistake as an entrepreneur can cost
you millions of dollars, momentum, and years

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.040
of lost time.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.570
And the reason why I tell you this is because
a lot of times marketers, when you think about

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:15.549
marketers, there are a lot of mistakes you
can make as a marketer.

00:00:15.549 --> 00:00:18.029
And by the way, if you're an entrepreneur,
CEO, guess what else you are?

00:00:18.029 --> 00:00:21.270
You are somewhat of a marketer, because at
the beginning when you're building your company,

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:27.450
as a founder, you may not know it, but you're
marketing, whether it's directly or indirectly,

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:28.750
you're marketing yourself.

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:33.640
And you don't ever want, as a marketer to
have a bipolar personality.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.649
Just like individuals sometimes one day we're
happy, one day we're upset, one day we're

00:00:36.649 --> 00:00:37.969
this, one day we're that.

00:00:37.969 --> 00:00:40.629
Sometimes you see some brands that they're
so bipolar.

00:00:40.629 --> 00:00:41.629
They're all over the place.

00:00:41.629 --> 00:00:42.629
They don't even know what it is.

00:00:42.629 --> 00:00:47.309
It's almost like a kid that wants to try to
please everybody.

00:00:47.309 --> 00:00:54.309
And the highest form of maturity is when one
becomes independent of others' opinions, meaning

00:00:54.309 --> 00:00:58.429
you as a CEO, you as an entrepreneur, need
to position yourself and realize who you want

00:00:58.429 --> 00:00:59.429
to be and sticking to it.

00:00:59.429 --> 00:01:07.320
Here's why the biggest mistake marketers will
make is trying to appeal to every single customer

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.289
and you just can't do that.

00:01:09.289 --> 00:01:14.640
Because no one hasn't done it and become massively
big, because you just can't do it.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:15.640
Now why is that?

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.630
Let's talk about it a little bit for you to
understand what it is.

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:23.390
Look, there's really four different categories
you can choose to be, as an entrepreneur,

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:24.390
and as a business.

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:25.390
Let me explain.

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:29.659
On this side [left], we have high quality,
meaning the higher you are here, represents

00:01:29.659 --> 00:01:32.010
the better quality of a product you have.

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.880
If you're further on the pendulum here means
you're low cost.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:39.020
Cost, more expensive [right], less expensive
[ left].

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:40.730
Better quality [top], lower quality [bottom].

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:43.590
So if your product is here [bottom left],
it means you're what?

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:45.270
Low cost, low quality.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:49.049
If your product falls here [top right], you're
high quality, high cost.

00:01:49.049 --> 00:01:51.990
If you're here [top left], quality is high,
but cost is low.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.490
If you're here [bottom right], quality is
low and cost is high.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:58.409
Now some people may say, "Pat, can't I just
be everywhere?"

00:01:58.409 --> 00:01:59.780
No, you can't.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:00.930
It's very simple.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.810
Harvard is here [top right].

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:05.549
A community college is here [bottom left].

00:02:05.549 --> 00:02:10.620
Public education may be here, but some places,
if you go and live, I don't know how many

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:11.620
times.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:15.610
. . I'm going to try to get my kids into West
Plano area, because it has the great what?

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:17.780
High quality education, but what?

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:18.900
Low cost.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.170
That's still a bracket you're going into.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:23.700
You go into certain areas, and public school's
going to be here [bottom left].

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.490
You go in some private schools and may be
up here [top right].

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:26.870
It doesn't matter what the product is.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:28.450
I can go into different products.

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:31.050
Let's look over here, and I want to do a test
with you.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.050
Ferrari.

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:33.050
Think about it.

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:36.080
Which of these four categories do you think
Ferrari falls under?

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:38.680
A low quality, low cost?

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:39.680
No.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:40.680
Low quality high cost?

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.430
It's not low quality so we know automatically
it's not here [bottom].

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:44.430
Is it high quality?

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:45.430
Yes.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:47.150
Do we know it's high quality, high cost?

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:48.450
Yes, Ferrari is right here [top right].

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:49.850
How about Starbucks?

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:55.050
You pay $5 for a cappuccino, whatever you
want to call a Frappuccino, so cost is high

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.050
and quality is high.

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:57.090
At least that's what they claim.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:00.540
And if their quality wasn't that high, they
wouldn't be as big as they are today.

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:02.130
So they must have great quality.

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:04.550
If we go to Tesla, what do you think Tesla
is?

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.860
Is Tesla very, very expensive?

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:10.820
So you may say, no, the cost is not really
that high, but the quality is high.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:12.850
So maybe Tesla is here [top left].

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:14.160
Maybe Tesla is here.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:19.060
Because you can get a Tesla nowadays, what
Elon Musk's goal is to try to produce a product

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:22.200
that's a middle America product, with a very,
very high quality.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.840
He wants to go here [top left].

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:24.840
That's his goal.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:26.050
He's not trying to be Ferrari.

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:28.850
He wants to be here, high quality, low cost.

00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:29.870
Where's Wal-Mart?

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:30.870
Think about Wal-Mart.

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:33.640
Do you think Wal-Mart cares that they're here
[bottom left]?

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:35.600
Wal-Mart's the biggest company in the world.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:38.760
Wal-Mart doesn't care if you don't like Wal-Mart.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:43.420
Wal-Mart knows, we're not going to sell $6,000
Canali suits, and we're okay with it.

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:46.890
You're not going to go to Wal-Mart and say,
"I would like to buy some Ferragamo shoes."

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.370
They're going to say, "I'm sorry, you want
what kind of shoes?

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:51.710
We got a Ferrajack shoe, for $9.99.

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:53.410
You're not getting Ferragamo shoes here."

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:55.390
I'd like to get a Ferragamo belt.

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:56.580
Ferra what?

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:57.580
Ferra.

00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:59.980
. . We have Ferra belts, $11.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:01.770
Forget the gamo, we have the Ferra belt.

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:03.840
You're just not going to get that.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.440
And that's where Wal-Mart plays.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:07.690
And by the way, they have 2.2 million employees
around the world.

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:10.700
So if you say, "I don't like Wal-Mart," they
don't care if you like them.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:12.480
That's where they are [bottom left].

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:13.480
Jaguar.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:15.770
Some people say, why did you put Jaguar on
there?

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:18.880
Because Jaguar is low quality, high cost.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:24.900
Before they become Ford, for many years, everybody
would make fun of Jaguar, because Jaguar wanted

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:33.870
to be this high end, luxury car that you pay
$60,000 to drive, or $55,000, $80,000 to drive,

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.720
and it looked good, but the quality just wasn't
that good.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.570
It was very expensive, low quality.

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:44.660
Until Ford bought Jaguar, and they changed
their reputation into what they are right

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:45.660
now.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:46.780
If they didn't, they would be still here.

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:48.360
By the way, a IKEA would be what?

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:51.350
IKEA would probably be low quality, low cost.

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:55.460
IKEA would like to say we're here [top left],
but they're here [bottom left].

00:04:55.460 --> 00:05:00.950
Now, for myself, when we started the company,
I started off, myself, as Morgan Stanley Dean

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:01.950
Witter.

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:02.950
.Now watch this.

00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:05.260
When I started with Morgan Stanley Dean Witter,
I just want to tell you this.

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:09.200
The clientele you dealt with at Morgan Stanley
Dean Witter were who?

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.190
People that had a net worth of a million dollars
and up.

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:14.330
So I'm dealing purely here [top right].

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:16.980
Our fees were higher, but I'm also up here
[top right].

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:20.400
Then I went to Trans, then I started PHP,
with our friends.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.050
We started the PHP Agency.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:27.580
Our goal was to be high quality, low cost.

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:29.130
This is where we wanted to be.

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:33.940
We want to provide the highest quality products
out there, with the lowest amount of cost,

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:35.430
that works for us.

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:43.240
Now, anytime you create an identity, anytime
you create an identity, once you create an

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:46.520
identity, you have a reputation of what your
product is.

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:51.120
When you have a reputation of what your product
is, and all of a sudden you decide to change

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:52.930
it, guess who you throw off?

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:55.170
Your existing base of customers.

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:56.170
Because here's what happens.

00:05:56.170 --> 00:06:00.480
The thought is, well, you know what, if you
try to appeal to these guys, we'll also do

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:01.480
a very, very good job.

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:03.430
Can you imagine how much money we could do?

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:06.010
Because it makes sense, right, common sense
wise.

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:09.540
You know, we need to get everybody because
it just makes a lot of sense.

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:10.540
Really?

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:12.140
Let me tell you a little bit of history.

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:15.460
There used to be a time there was competition
for cigarettes.

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:17.160
Many, many years ago.

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:20.680
You had Marlboro, Camel, and Winston who were
competing.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:21.680
Very close.

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:23.180
If you remember, Camel was about what?

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:26.520
How cool they wanted to be, all this other
stuff, and Winston was coming up, all these

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:28.419
things, and Marlboro.

00:06:28.419 --> 00:06:29.419
Okay?

00:06:29.419 --> 00:06:35.670
What ended up happening during this period,
Camel and Winston fell for market manipulation,

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:40.520
media criticism, all this stuff, and this
is what the market did to them, media did.

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:46.410
Media told Winston, Camel, and Marlboro, you
know what your mistake is?

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:47.780
You don't appeal to women.

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:49.479
That was a big.

00:06:49.479 --> 00:06:51.480
. . believe it, you're watching this right
now and saying, "Pat, what are you talking

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:52.480
about?"

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:53.480
Go study it.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:54.630
You don't appeal to women.

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:59.690
People are not attracted to Marlboro, Winston,
and Camel, and we believe, these are consultants,

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:01.389
those who know everything, right?

00:07:01.389 --> 00:07:06.990
We believe if you try to start appealing to
women, you're going to crush it, and the first

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:09.170
one to do that is going to blow up.

00:07:09.170 --> 00:07:10.170
Really?

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:11.170
So guess what happens?

00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:13.380
Camel and Winston fell for it.

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:16.080
They started having girls smoking cigarettes
in commercials.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:18.800
They started having girls holding cigarettes
in magazines.

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:21.450
They started having all these pretty girls
smoking cigarettes.

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:22.450
And they said, Oh, we're going to get it.

00:07:22.450 --> 00:07:23.450
We're so smart.

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:24.960
Marlboro is not moving.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:25.960
They don't move.

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:26.960
They don't move.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:27.960
They don't move.

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:28.960
And guess what ended up happening?

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:29.960
They never moved.

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:30.970
Marlboro didn't move.

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:32.650
Marlboro didn't fall for anything.

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:36.470
Because Marlboro made a decision a long time
ago, Marlboro is a cigarette, this is how

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:38.780
they are, and they're completely okay with
that.

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:42.260
Marlboro is a cigarette for man's man.

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:43.860
That's their m.o.

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:46.500
While everybody told them to switch, they
didn't do it.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:47.500
Now watch what happened.

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:48.500
Let me give you some numbers here.

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:54.170
Today in the marketplace, Winston has 2% of
the market.

00:07:54.170 --> 00:07:57.180
Camel has 8% of the market.

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:00.320
Newport has 13% of the market.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:02.620
And let me tell you what Marlboro's got.

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:03.620
Let me tell you here.

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:05.110
So we got Winston what, 2?

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:06.500
8 [Camel], 13 [Newport].

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:08.979
You know what Marlboro's got?

00:08:08.979 --> 00:08:12.210
41% goes to Marlboro!

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:13.350
You know why?

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:14.540
Here's why.

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:17.820
Now it's cigarettes, by the way, just so you
know, the brand alone, Marlboro, if you wanted

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:22.480
to buy it, it's $80 billion, if you want to
buy it today.

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:29.780
Last year, 391 billion Marlboro cigarettes
were smoked, last year.

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:31.560
Let me say it again.

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:32.979
391 billion cigarettes.

00:08:32.979 --> 00:08:34.060
I don't smoke cigarettes.

00:08:34.060 --> 00:08:35.800
I can't stand cigarettes.

00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:37.249
There's nothing about cigarettes I like.

00:08:37.249 --> 00:08:38.459
My family, they were smokers.

00:08:38.459 --> 00:08:41.000
I cannot stand cigarettes to save my life.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:44.480
I'll smoke four cigars per year, and that's
pretty much it.

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:47.149
I cannot stand the smell of cigarettes.

00:08:47.149 --> 00:08:48.149
41%.

00:08:48.149 --> 00:08:49.149
Why?

00:08:49.149 --> 00:08:51.369
They stuck to their product.

00:08:51.369 --> 00:08:53.380
So the question comes back to you here.

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:55.509
This is me and you talking now, nobody else.

00:08:55.509 --> 00:08:57.779
I got the question for you..

00:08:57.779 --> 00:09:00.180
Who is your ideal customer?

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:02.490
Who is your ideal customer?

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:04.930
Are you trying to please everybody?

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:10.959
Can you imagine if Morgan Stanley one day
says, "yes, our ideal client is a net-worth

00:09:10.959 --> 00:09:15.360
of $5 million, with a $1 million of investible
cash.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:18.879
And oh, by the way, you can also do $50 a
month with Morgan Stanley Dean Witter.

00:09:18.879 --> 00:09:20.459
What are you talking about?

00:09:20.459 --> 00:09:23.680
I go to the bank to do that.

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:25.769
I go somewhere to open up a CD.

00:09:25.769 --> 00:09:27.279
You want to do that [top right].

00:09:27.279 --> 00:09:28.279
No way.

00:09:28.279 --> 00:09:29.279
They lose their identity.

00:09:29.279 --> 00:09:31.200
And by the way, so many companies do this.

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:35.600
So if you're a smaller company and you're
thinking, you know, it's just me.

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:41.040
. . so many people, because they see the potential
of the market and they want everything.

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:42.860
It is very important.

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:45.279
What if Wal-Mart, look how confusing this
could be.

00:09:45.279 --> 00:09:46.570
I want you to think about it.

00:09:46.570 --> 00:09:50.879
Wal-Mart makes an announcement next year,
and this is what Wal-Mart says.

00:09:50.879 --> 00:09:58.040
"Effective January 1st 2017, Wal-Mart will
start selling Ferragamo and Canali suits."

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:00.720
What would you say if Wal-Mart said that?

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:02.050
Tell me you wouldn't be confused.

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:06.709
And by the way, do you think the customers
that go to Neiman Marcus, which is high-quality,

00:10:06.709 --> 00:10:11.670
high-cost, you think they would go into Wal-Mart
to go buy those Canali suits?

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:14.040
Do you know how confusing it is?

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:15.529
It's very confusing.

00:10:15.529 --> 00:10:17.119
Very, very confusing.

00:10:17.119 --> 00:10:22.319
It's the same to say if Neiman Marcus decided
to say, "We're selling $9 shoes, come and

00:10:22.319 --> 00:10:23.319
get it."

00:10:23.319 --> 00:10:24.819
That's just not going to happen.

00:10:24.819 --> 00:10:26.100
So who's you're ideal customer?

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:27.100
You.

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:28.510
You've got to ask the question, do you know
it?

00:10:28.510 --> 00:10:32.490
Age bracket, income range, what do are they
doing, what do they like, why do they come

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:33.490
to you?

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:34.740
Who is your product?

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:36.700
What are you marketing your product to?

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:38.269
And then stick to it.

00:10:38.269 --> 00:10:44.509
And do whatever you can to compete in that
space and be the best in that space.

00:10:44.509 --> 00:10:49.490
So your biggest differentiator could be Neiman
Marcus and Nordstrom [top right], we are here

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:50.540
[bottom left], and we're happy with that.

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:52.700
We are here [top left], we're happy with that.

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:53.700
You can go here [bottom right].

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:54.700
We're going over here [bottom left].

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:57.110
Target likes to say Target is whatever, but
guess what?

00:10:57.110 --> 00:11:00.749
Target is not as low cost as Wal-Mart is.

00:11:00.749 --> 00:11:03.559
And by the way, here's the other part to keep
in mind about this.

00:11:03.559 --> 00:11:06.300
Once you decide who your ideal customer is,
and once you decide where you want to position

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:09.839
yourself, don't let this confuse you.

00:11:09.839 --> 00:11:10.860
You will have enemies.

